re, Singapore (NUS) and other places (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Yanpei-Guo-2316425177)\nciteturn3search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Celer: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries ... Lookup arguments are designed to prove that all elements in a query vector appear in a lookup table. ... Conference Paper\n\n# Yanpei Guo’s research while affiliated with National University of Singapore and other places\n\n## What is this page?\n\nThis page lists works of an author who doesn't have a ResearchGate profile or hasn't added the works to their profile yet. It is automatically generated from public (personal) data to further our legitimate goal of comprehensive and accurate scientific recordkeeping. If you are this author and want this page removed, please let us know.\n\n## Publications (7)\n\nCeler: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries\n\nChapter\n\nAugust 2026\n\nLecture Notes in Computer Science\n\nWenjie Qu\n\n·\n\nYanpei Guo\n\n·\n\nZhen Xuan\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nJiaheng Zhang\n\nLookup arguments are designed to prove that all elements in a query vector appear in a lookup table. These arguments are critical for efficiently proving ZK-unfriendly statements, such as range checks and the evaluation of complex functions. Most existing lookup...\n\nVerfCNN, Optimal Complexity zkSNARK for Convolutional Neural Networks\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLogup* (https://georgwiese.github.io/crypto-summaries/Papers/Logup%2A)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Jun 30, 2026 2 min read ... An indexed lookup argument where the looked-up values are virtual (not committed). Especially efficient for small tables (m≪n). ...   * A table T (size m)\n\n  * # Logup*\n\nJun 30, 2026 2 min read\n\nePrint 2025/946 | Lev Soukhanov, 2025\n\nAn indexed lookup argument where the looked-up values are virtual (not committed). Especially efficient for small tables (m≪n).\n\n## Setting\n\nGiven commitments to:\n\n  * An index array I (size n)\n  * A table T (size m)\n\nGoal: evaluate the pullback (I∗T)[i]:=T[I[i]] at a random point r, without committing to I∗T.\n\n## Pushforward\n\nThe dual of the pullback. Given I:{0,…,n−1}→{0,…,m−1} and A∈F n:\n\nI∗​A[j]=i∣I[i]=j∑​A[i]\n\nDuality lemma: ⟨I∗​A,B⟩=⟨A,I∗B⟩.\n\n## Protocol\n\nTrade the pullback for a pushforward using the duality lemma:\n\nI∗T(r)=⟨I∗T,eq r​⟩=⟨T,I∗​eq r​⟩\n\nwhere eq r​∈F ext n​ is the vector of Lagrange basis evaluations at r.\n\nSteps:\n\n  1. Prover commits to I∗​eq r​∈F ext m​.\n  2. Run sum-check for ⟨T,I∗​eq r​⟩=e, obtaining evaluation claims for T and I∗​eq r​​.\n  3. Open T and I∗​eq r​ via polynomial commitments.\n\nWell-formedness of I∗​eq r​ is proven via GKR, using a LogUp-style fractional sum:\n\n0≤i<n∑​c−I[i]eq r​[i]​=0≤j<m∑​c−j I∗​eq r​[j]​\n\nThe verifier can evaluate eq​r​ by itself, so no commitment to it is needed.\n\n## Cost comparison vs. standard LogUp\n\nCompared to LogUp + GKR with the standard indexed-to-unindexed reduction:\n\n  * Saves: commitment to I∗T (n base field elements, multiplied by t for tuple lookups of size t) and multiplicities (m elements)\n  * Adds: commitment to I∗​eq r​ (m extension field elements) + extra sum-checks\n\nBest when m≪n and table values are large (extension field elements or tuples). Also avoids the numerator overflow issue that affects standard LogUp accumulators. For example, it can be useful in Spark.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIACR ePrint 2026 — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/venue/iacr-eprint/2026)\nciteturn3search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Directions are Lacuna’s clustering of the concept elements extracted from each paper, so a paper can belong to several and direction counts add up to more than the paper count. ... Celer: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries\nA SNARK for (Non-)Subsequences with Text-Sub-